-- Subtract isolated cylindrical through-holes analytically from single prismatic modeling bodies. Check disk clearance and material winding, reuse exact plane/cylinder sections, and retain every unaffected face. Native circular extrusions and line/circle cap boundaries are supported; contacts, overlapping or blind cuts, cavities, multiple shells and unsupported geometry use the existing Boolean solver. Regressions cover transformed sequences, reversed axes, concave outlines, thin walls, STEP export and subsequent general cuts.
+- Reuse predicted surface parameters when checking sampled curve inclusion, including fillet boundaries. Keep all 33 samples per edge and validate finite parameters, parameter ranges and geometric residuals before accepting a seeded solve; fall back to the existing global search on failure or across seams.
+- Extend analytic through-hole subtraction to batches of disjoint parallel cylindrical cutters. Verify complete outward cutter shells and separation, collect all openings before rebuilding the cap faces, and preserve unaffected faces and material-removal reporting. Unsupported or ambiguous batches use the general Boolean solver.
+- Subtract isolated cylindrical through-holes analytically from single prismatic modeling bodies. Check disk clearance and material winding, reuse exact plane/cylinder sections, and retain every unaffected face. Native circular extrusions and line/circle cap boundaries are supported; contacts, overlapping or blind cuts, cavities, multiple target shells and unsupported geometry use the existing Boolean solver. Regressions cover transformed sequences, reversed axes, concave outlines, thin walls, STEP export and subsequent general cuts.
 - Breaking: `subtract`, `subtract_with_effect`, `try_subtract` and `try_subtract_with_effect` now require `'static` curve and surface types so modeling solids can use analytic subtraction. Generic wrappers must add `C: 'static, S: 'static`; borrowed geometry must become owned. Borrowing the solid itself is unchanged. PiezaCAD's calls in `cad-core/src/kernel/evaluation.rs` use owned modeling types and need no changes.
 
 - Preserve collapsed tessellation trim loops by subdividing the original curve parameters locally, independently of chord tolerance. Only affected shared edges and their incident faces are resampled; unrelated boundaries retain their mesh. Planar trims never fall back to the full surface domain. Arc/chord faces, spline lenses and tiny holes are covered at coarse tolerances, across transforms and serial, parallel and compressed tessellation. Invalid or unresolvable trims remain missing faces reported by the existing diagnostics, with bounded recovery work.
